@startuml
' Where the tokens actually go on a retrieval, and what each layer costs.
' Plain sequence rather than C4: this is about ORDER and COST, not structure.
skinparam backgroundColor transparent
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam ParticipantPadding 12
skinparam BoxPadding 8

title Progressive disclosure - one traversal, two ways to pay for it

participant "Agent session" as agent
participant "AgentHive" as api
database "Approved graph" as db

== The default: one call, every body ==

agent -> api : retrieve_context(anchor)
api -> db : approved nodes for this team
db --> api : nodes
api -> api : bounded traversal\n(hops, hub cutoff)
api --> agent : neighbourhood + every body\n**~2,300 tokens for 5 nodes**

note right of agent
  One round trip, nothing wasted on
  a second call. The right default -
  and it bills for bodies the agent
  would have skipped given titles.
end note

== The alternative: see the price before paying it ==

agent -> api : retrieve_context(anchor, mode=index)
api -> api : the SAME traversal,\nserved from the SAME cache entry
api --> agent : ids, titles, tiers, tags,\nper-node token cost\n**~40 tokens**

note right of agent
  Now the agent can choose. Tags
  narrow it further without reading
  a single body.
end note

agent -> api : fetch_nodes([one id])
api -> db : approved nodes for this team
db --> api : nodes
api --> agent : just that body\n**~460 tokens**

note over agent, api
  **500 against 2,300.** The saving is the four bodies never sent.
  An agent that fetches all five has saved nothing and spent an
  extra round trip - which is why retrieve_context stays the
  default, and why the metrics report both halves separately.
end note

@enduml
